civil_code.amendment_definition_eng:
(1) The seller has an obligation to deliver goods without defects to the buyer.

(2) Unless otherwise agreed by the parties, the goods delivered to the buyer shall be deemed defective in any of the following cases:

a) the goods do not conform to the quantity, quality, or description specified in the contract;

b) the goods differ from the sample or model shown by the seller to the buyer in terms of quantity, quality, or description;

c) the goods are not suitable for the particular purpose expressly or implicitly indicated by the seller at the time the contract was made;

d) the goods are not suitable for the ordinary use of goods of the same type;

e) the goods are not packaged in the usual manner for such goods or in a proper way to protect and preserve them.

Husband and wife shall be jointly liable for the following obligations:

a) Expenses necessary for maintaining the joint household of the husband and wife, and expenses for the education, medical care, and other needs of their children;

b) Obligations and other liabilities jointly agreed to in writing by both spouses with the creditor during the marriage, or obligations and other liabilities undertaken by one spouse with the written consent of the other spouse during the marriage;

c) Expenses for the management and preservation of common property.

Except in cases of urgent necessity, the parent holding parental authority shall consult with the other parent who does not hold parental authority regarding the following matters relating to the child:

a) The child’s education beyond the level of compulsory education as defined by law;

b) Change of the child’s residence from one capital or province to another;

c) The child’s profession or business;

d) Medical treatment affecting the child’s life;

e) Disposition of the child’s significant property;

f) Other important matters related to the care and management of the child and the child’s property.

(1) The co-successors may commence consultation on the partition of the estate at any time after one (1) month has passed from the commencement of succession;
however, the change of ownership of the estate resulting from the partition must comply with the provisions of Article 1277 (Prohibition of change of ownership), and if the decedent has prohibited partition in a will, partition cannot be carried out during the prohibited period.

(2) In order to transfer rights to property through partition, the partition must be made in the form required for the transfer of each specific right, such as in writing or other necessary formalities.
